        ("What does Shannon Entropy measure, and how is it mathematically defined?",
         "Shannon Entropy measures the fundamental limit of uncertainty, unpredictability, or average information content produced by a discrete stochastic source. For a random variable $X$ with probability distribution $P(X)$:\n$$H(X) = - \\sum_{x \\in \\mathcal{X}} P(x) \\log_2 P(x)$$\nIntuitively, the 'surprise' or self-information of an event is $I(x) = \\log_2(1 / P(x))$. Highly probable events convey little information (zero surprise), whereas rare events carry immense information. Entropy is the expected value of surprise.\n\nA fair coin has maximum entropy $H = 1$ bit (maximum uncertainty), whereas a double-headed coin has $H = 0$ bits (zero uncertainty)."),
        ("What is Kullback-Leibler (KL) Divergence, and why is it not a true mathematical distance metric?",
         "KL Divergence (relative entropy) measures how much information is lost when approximating a true probability distribution $P$ with an alternative distribution $Q$:\n$$D_{KL}(P \\parallel Q) = \\sum_{x} P(x) \\log \\left(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\right)$$\nIt is not a true distance metric because it violates two fundamental metric axioms:\n1. Non-symmetry: $D_{KL}(P \\parallel Q) \\ne D_{KL}(Q \\parallel P)$.\n2. Triangle inequality: It does not satisfy the triangle inequality.\n\nIn deep learning, minimizing cross-entropy loss is mathematically equivalent to minimizing the KL divergence from the true data distribution to the model's predicted distribution.")
