        ("How does the geometric concept of orthogonal projection solve the Linear Least Squares problem?",
         "In linear regression and parameter estimation, we often have an overdetermined linear system $A x = b$ where matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ has more rows than columns ($m > n$), meaning vector $b$ does not lie within the column space $\\text{Col}(A)$. Consequently, no exact solution $x$ exists.\n\nTo find the optimal approximate solution $\\hat{x}$ that minimizes the squared residual error $\\|A x - b\\|^2$, we project $b$ orthogonally onto the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$. The error vector $e = b - A\\hat{x}$ must be strictly orthogonal to every column vector in $A$."),
        ("Can you derive the Normal Equations from that orthogonality condition?",
         "Since the error vector $b - A\\hat{x}$ is orthogonal to all columns of $A$, its inner product with each column must be zero, which in matrix form is:\n$$A^T (b - A\\hat{x}) = 0$$\nExpanding this:\n$$A^T b - A^T A \\hat{x} = 0 \\implies A^T A \\hat{x} = A^T b$$\nThese are the celebrated Normal Equations. If $A$ has full column rank, $A^T A$ is invertible, yielding the unique least-squares solution:\n$$\\hat{x} = (A^T A)^{-1} A^T b$$\nThe matrix $(A^T A)^{-1} A^T$ is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse $A^+$.")
